====Usage notes====
* The term ''[[transgenderism]]'' was used in 1965.<ref name="oliven"/><ref name="Bevan">Thomas E. Bevan, ''The Psychobiology of Transsexualism and Transgenderism'' (2014, {{ISBN|1440831270}}, [https://archive.org/details/psychobiologyoft0000beva/page/42/ pages 42, 61]: "The term transsexual was introduced by Cauldwell (1949) and popularized by Harry Benjamin (1966) [...]. The term transgender was coined by John Oliven (1965) and was popularized by various transgender people who pioneered the concept and practice of transgenderism.{{...}} many transgender people [who] advocated the use of the term much more than Prince. [...] Transsexuals constitute a subset of transgender people.{{...}}Oliven, J. (1965, June). Transgenderism = transsexualism. ''Sexual Hygiene and Pathology'', 514.</ref> ''Transgender'' was popularized in the 1970s,<ref name="Merriam-Webster Online">{{R:Merriam-Webster Online}}</ref><ref name="Dict">{{R:Dictionary.com}}</ref> and by the 1990s it had largely displaced the older term {{m|en|transsexual}}.<ref name="TR2006">''Transgender Rights'' (2006, {{ISBN|0816643121}}, edited by Paisley Currah, Richard M. Juang, Shannon Minter; page 4: "From signifying a subject position between cross-dresser and transsexual, the meaning of ''transgender'' expanded radically in the early 1990s to include them, along with other cross-gender practices and identities."</ref><ref>{{cite-web|work=Google Books Ngram Viewer
|title=transgender,transsexual
|url=https://books.google.com/ngrams/graph?content=transgender%2Ctranssexual&year_start=1960&year_end=2013&corpus=15&smoothing=3&share=&direct_url=t1%3B%2Ctransgender%3B%2Cc0%3B.t1%3B%2Ctranssexual%3B%2Cc0
|accessdate=December 5, 2017}}</ref> (''Transsexual'' is now often considered outdated<ref name="Dict"/> although some people still prefer it; see {{l|en|transsexual|its entry}} for more. Neither term should be confused with {{m|en|transvestite}}; see that entry for more.) ''Transgender'' is an umbrella term, encompassing [[trans man|trans men]] and [[trans woman|trans women]], and often also encompassing nonbinary people;<ref>{{cite-book|title=The SAGE Encyclopedia of Filipina/x/o American Studies|year=2022|editor1=Kevin Leo Yabut Nadal|editor2=‎Allyson Tintiangco-Cubales|editor3=E.J.R. David|text=Transgender is an umbrella term used to describe people whose gender identity or lived experience does not match their sex assigned at birth. It can be used to describe people who were assigned a binary sex at birth [...] and who identify with the other binary sex [...]. ''Transgender'' can also be used to identify people who identify with a nonbinary gender.}}</ref> using ''transgender'' to refer strictly to people with a binary gender identity is often now considered offensive and exclusionary towards non-binary people.
* For the usage of this word (and similar adjectives) as a noun, see below.<!--This note is necessary because several other pages point to this page's usage notes, meaning the notes below rather than the etymological notes here.-->

